A||||51||Shiner||Street Races||||12
Z||000000||Shiner||09-22-1999||03:10 AM||||I KNOW I would get booted from Fiero.org's list for this post, so I am putting it here.<P> I drive an '87 GT 5speed, which I never street raced.  I always hear about fiero's taking this and that car to the cleaners.  Well, most of the people on this board are honest.  I want to know what you can REALLY smoke in a fiero.  I want to hear TRUE stories.  After I get it fixed up a bit, I am going to go looking for races in a local riceboy hotspot.  Of course I will only race in a closed race track.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> I LOVE to hear fiero stories, so gimmie some good stuff here guys!<P>Warren<BR>'87 GT 5speed<BR>'84 SC auto ||||reg||12
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-22-1999||09:27 AM||||Here's how I've fared lately...<P>Wins:<BR>Honda Civic Si hatchback (1994?)<BR>Mustang V6 (so new it still had window stickers)<BR>BMW 318i (the newer chopped-off hatch kind)<BR>Mitsubishi Eclipse (DOHC, non-turbo)<BR>Pontiac Sunfire GT<BR>Several Jettas and Golfs<BR>More little rice-boy Toyota/Honda/Nissan sedans with booming systems and coffee-can exhausts than I can remember<P>Neck-and-Neck, Too Close To Call:<BR>Chevy Lumina Z34<BR>Honda Civic Si sedan (1999)<P>Losses:<BR>Toyota Supra Turbo (late 80s)<BR>Several V8 Mustangs<BR>Cadillac Seville STS (had 32V Northstar badge, which I could clearly read as he pulled away)<BR>Honda Prelude VTEC (mid 90's rounded kind)<BR>Nissan 300ZX Twin-turbo (this one wasn't even close)<P>I have an 86 V6 SE 4-speed.  Most of these races were from a standing start, 1/8 mile or less.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fab4mula||09-22-1999||10:37 AM||||I've got an 88 Formula and a 99 Integra GSR.  My Teg will eat my Formula for lunch.  The Formula's a neater car, but there's something about a 1.8 liter motor putting out 170hp with an 8000 RPM redline.  Still, I think the Formula's the neater of the two, and I'll still have that long after the Teg's been traded for another daily driver.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  The GT should outrun a Teg LS/RS/GS, but forget about the Type-R or GSR.  Unless you've got more than a stock GT.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-22-1999||06:13 PM||||Fab..you did not indicate whether your Formula is a 5-Speed or Automatic... Also, The GT Fiero has the same engine design and horsepower as the Formula as well as the gear ratios in the Tranny... The Fiero Formula is a Rally car in which is totally lighter than the Fiero GT...So your Formula should pose no threat to any import cars.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||09-22-1999||07:57 PM||||UH. . . What? Lighter than a GT but no threat??? Uh. . .||||reg||5
Z||000005||Formula||09-22-1999||07:57 PM||||why would the formula pose no threat? I'm just curious because mine is an auto. And what is meant by rally car? Does that mean its better on a dirt track like sega rally? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||09-22-1999||07:58 PM||||whoa same time cooter!||||reg||1
Z||000008||88 Fiero||09-22-1999||09:30 PM||||Formula....the Fiero Formula is what they called a sleeper...because it has the body of a notch back coupe but have the basics of the GT Fiero...just like a Trans Am with a 350V8 Tune Port Injection and the Firebird Formula with the same engine...back in late eithies..the Formula is a choice for rally races because it's light. If you look at the history of the Fiero in Racing. On the Rally part all you can see are notch back coupe. Why the Notch Backs??? because you are talking at least 200 lbs. of weight difference from the GT Fastback....My 88 use to have the Formula and I have race GT Fiero's and did not even come very close in beating me...I have a 5-Speed...I've race integra's including the GSR's from traffic light to traffic light and did not even come close... and this is with the Formula body in it...Now it has the GT Fasbacks...Why change it??? co's the fastbacks have the looks...that's my opinion...<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 09-22-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||09-23-1999||03:51 AM||||The formula/GT weight difference is very debatable.  The actual rear clip is NOT that much bigger in reality.  Look at it.  I can't find any reason why the rear light assys. would weigh more.  The car does have a couple pounds more than the formula in the ground effects area.  The GT does have more standard equipment though, power windows and doors and things.  Formula's tend to be more basic cars.  However, I can't see this adding up to 200 lbs.  I have looked many places, and they all put the GT right at 2700 pounds, give or take 50.  Now, you are telling me that a formula weighs as much as my '84 SC??? There is no way.||||reg||5
Z||000010||mwbackus||09-23-1999||06:51 AM||||I myself doubt that the weight difference between a Formula and an 88 GT is enough to explain the moderate performance advantage of the formula seen in the various tests of the respective cars.  However, it is a well known fact that some of the 2.8's dynoed from 150 - 160 hp at the factory, and I believe that the Formula would have been the more likely candidate for these engines.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Fiero||09-23-1999||10:06 AM||||Shiner...have you seen the Witzenburg book about the Fiero's..you probably want to check that you and compare it yourself...Plus.. Fiero Phil from FOCOA also indicated the weight difference of the Coupe Fiero against the GT...The GT's have the big Buttress in the rear and it does make a difference as far as weight...Also, this have been confirmed when the club went to the Pontiac Assembly Plant for Fiero's....||||reg||1
Z||000012||88 Fiero||09-23-1999||10:16 AM||||Guys!!! I love this Forum...the best!!! We might have different opinion on things about our cars...but we still share the best of it and not reaping each others head off!!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||DXR_DAD||09-23-1999||03:57 PM||||Guys I love it also!!!!! but what I want know is; is the 86 SE the start of the GT? all standard equip. seems to be the same, power, extra gauge set, front fascia and so-on. what is the weight of the SE vs GT? and hp||||reg||5
Z||000014||88 Fiero||09-23-1999||04:21 PM||||DXR...I will scan the Witzenburg page and post it in here..This page will tell you the different Curb weights of the Fiero's in different Years and Models, also the Tranny gear Ratio's, Specs of the car....||||reg||1
Z||000015||GT Bastard||09-23-1999||11:54 PM||||Just for your info..... My 87 GT weighs exactly 2708 lbs.   Mine is an automatic with a wing(if that makes a difference.) As far as racing goes, I think I'd MUCH rather take my 94Z to a race.  The Fiero is fun to screw around with, but it's lacking a little on the acceleration end of things...  It certainly gets the challenges though.  Once it gets going, it moves pretty well, but off the line it's EMBARASSING!  I'm used to taking off with a nifty smoke-show, but the Fiero does nothing.  Of course, the weight is all on the rear wheels.  And it just looks do DAMN COOL!!!!||||reg||3
Z||000016||mwbackus||09-24-1999||03:23 AM||||Thats strange... my Formula has no problem smoking my brand new BFG 245's at all..   Maybe a tune-up would help? :-)||||reg||1
Z||000017||88 Fiero||09-24-1999||10:08 AM||||GT...is your car pretty much stock??? I know a guy out here in California got a '88 with an automatic..He also got a 3.4 camaro block and a custom turbo.. The Turbo charger unit came from a Nissan 300ZX but was sold as a kit by IFG... In the beginning this turbo package sucks!!! IFG don't know jack about turbo charging...engine went bye!! bye!!. Anyway, he brought it to Keith Huff of Fiero Motorsports and did research in using the ZX turbo unit...a 1 1/2yr. pass by, he got the car intercooled and now it could leave a 6 foot skidmark in the ground and it can move...tranny was modified too in which he include a shift kit...By the way...have you tried burning rubber on a 5-Speed Fiero...or race someone with the manual tranny Fiero...That might change your mind...back then when I have my 85 SE V6 4-Speed. I have a Camaro RS with a V8...he was at the end of my door, but unfortunately I ran out of gears but I gave that guy a good run...anyway...Happy Fieroing!!!||||reg||1
Z||000018||GT Bastard||09-24-1999||11:02 AM||||Mwbackus??? Is you're Formula an automatic?? Just curious, because everyone with automatics has said they're cars will not even churp the tires from a dead stop.  And I just gave it a tune-up, but I still need to have the engine timing checked to make it complete.  later....||||reg||1
Z||000019||mwbackus||09-24-1999||11:30 AM||||Actually GTB, I was just kidding with ya a bit (note the smiley).  My Formula is not exactly stock, and it is a 5spd.  You can check some of my mods in my user profile if you wish.  I did find it peculiar that you would compare a 94Z to a stock auto V6? <BR>Rest assured, that if you study what others here have done to their Fieros, and implement some of these fairly low cost solutions, your Z will be parked much more frequently on race days. BTW, a high stall converter and a shift kit should fix that "chirping" problem just fine.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000020||Shaun41178||09-24-1999||11:55 AM||||Ok Back to the original topic, ie "street races"  I have raced a few cars out on the street.  I should also mention that I have yet to be beaten on the street.  The track is another story though.  I have raced:<BR>99 T/A, LS1 from light<BR>89 Toyata Supra Turbo, Modified with at least exhaust from roll<BR>94-98 V6 Mustang from roll<BR>(2) 4th gen Firebirds with a 305, one was TPI the other was TBI both from a light<BR>9? Honda Accord from roll<P>Mods include: 70 hp shot of nitrous, ported exhaust manifolds, crane ignition and coil, underdrive pulley, custom intake with K&N cone filter.  I run 8.9 in the 1/8 and a best of 14.2 in the 1/4.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000021||Cliff Pennock||09-24-1999||12:24 PM||||I hardly ever street race, simply because I know most of those GTi, GSi, GTX, TD (etc.) cars will smoke me. My 86.5 GT is completely stock and has an auto tranny. My average 0-60mph times are 9.2s which isn't exactly slow, but it isn't superfast also. The only thing my GT is very good at is cornering. I hardly have to slow down for 90 degree corners and that's usually enough to get rid of any car that's trying to keep up with me.<P>I'm still thinking of doing some mods of some kind, but I do not want to go the "bigger engine" route. I'm thinking of installing better headers and a new camshaft. That should give me between 20-40 hp extra, I hope.||||reg||9
Z||000022||batboy||09-24-1999||05:12 PM||||One of the most common questions I get about my V8 Fiero is: What will it do? I've actually been driving the Batmobile more lately and on longer cruises. For months after I first bought the car, something go wrong or it would breakdown about every other test drive. The other night I did some 0 to 60 mph runs. My first try was over 7.5 seconds, but I did not power brake at all and the automatic tranny was shifting into 3 at 55 mph. The next two runs were both about 7 seconds using a 1500 rpm power brake launch and leaving the shifter in second gear so it won't shift into third before 60 mph.<P>A 7 second 0-60 is pretty quick, but I was disappointed. Thought it would be faster than that. There seems to be a lot of slippage in that transmission. Would a shift kit be a smart investment? I'm thinking: my 85 Fiero GT V6 with a 4 speed probably can do close to 7 second 0-60 runs. Right? No way! I tried it and only got a 9 second best time.||||reg||1
Z||000023||88 Fiero||09-24-1999||05:51 PM||||bat...why don't you install a shift kit that way you got control of the converter. you can always adust when you want your tranny to shift..this guy that I know with an '88 GT, auto tranny and shift kit (adjust the way he wants the tranny to shift) got a 3.4 engine and a turbo and his car does in 6 second range acceleration..||||reg||1
Z||000024||Shaun41178||09-24-1999||07:09 PM||||Batty: What kind of V8 do you got?  About how much hp and torque do you think it is making?  7 seconds to 60 seems kind of slow for a V8 Fiero.  You using a stopwatch or something?  You have a g-tech?  Just wondering cause I have a g-tech and it has told me that I have reached 60 in 5.6 seconds.  I would guess that your V8 makes more low end torque then my V6 with nitrous so your car I would think should be down in the low 6 second range at least.<BR>If you are timing with a stopwatch I wouldn't trust it.  The human error factor comes into play here and makes the so called times innaccurate.  Just my thoughts anyway.||||reg||5
Z||000025||batboy||09-24-1999||09:39 PM||||Shaun, yep...I'm timing with a stopwatch, and I know that's not super accurate. That's why I was saying about 7 seconds. The tranny seems to slip a lot, especially during the launch and shifts. Actually, I was not being very aggressive taking off, I'm still working the bugs out of the car and don't want to break it again.<P>The engine is a stock 350 out of a 1988 Chevy pickup. An Edelbrock Performer carburetor is the only extra. The exhaust is through factory manifolds and custom built pipes and glasspacks. Hooker 3 inch resonator tips top it off. The engine has been run hard, probably when it first got swapped into the Fiero. I'm guess not much over 200 hp and maybe 300 ft/lbs of torque.<P>I have a 1988 Z-28 Camaro 350 engine half built that I was going to use in my 1950 Chevy truck. But...instead I'm going to use it in my Fiero after I get the transmission built up stronger. I have to finish the suspension mods on the Batmobile too.<P>The Z-28 motor has four bolt main caps. I'm using Comp Cams roller cam, roller lifters, and roller rockers arms. I put in TRW forged flat-top pistons. The crank and rods have been totally rebuilt and balanced. The block was magnafluxed, bored 0.030" and decked. Now if I buy a pair of Dart aluminum heads and use those street rod shorty ceramic coated headers, I might be looking at close to 400 hp and maybe 450 ft/lbs of torque.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Racer 1||09-25-1999||12:47 AM||||Wasnt the 84 or 85 I cant remeber excact year the fiero indy pace cars the first GT looking v-6?||||reg||4
Z||000027||batboy||09-25-1999||02:37 AM||||Racer 1, the Indy Fieros were in 1984, I believe they were all 4 cylinders. The first V6 GT was in 1985, but they were a notchback style. All GT's afterwards were fastbacks.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Racer 1||09-25-1999||04:00 AM||||Thanks Batboy.Hey ill get the pic to ya tommorrow need to wash my baby then take a good picture of her!||||reg||6
Z||000029||batboy||09-25-1999||10:56 AM||||I don't know much about shift kits. It's basically a modification of the valve body, right? So, can that be done without removing the tranny? Is installing a shift kit something a shade-tree weekend mechanic can handle out in the driveway or is it best done at a transmission shop?<P>Even a tired ol' stock 350 ought to have enough torque to push a Fiero 0-60 mph in 6 seconds, I hope! Time for a tune-up, maybe. I'm thinking of getting a new Accel HEI coil, plugs, cap, rotor, and wires. If I'm lucky, I'll get this beast a little closer to Shaun's times. Dang kids and their nitrous. Wait until next spring when I get my new engine installed. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000030||Silver Bullet||09-26-1999||10:28 AM||||I have an 85 SE v6 auto, and here's how I have done.<P>   wins<BR>2 or 3 Civics (older)<BR>95 Eclipse (no turbo)<BR>6 cyl Camaro<BR>Nissan 300 ZX (it was a rag)<P>losses<P>92 MR2 Turbo(friend of mine)<BR>92 civic( i found out afterward this had a 230 hp GSR engine, no wonder!!)<P>I don't race unless I think I have at least a sporting chance.  When I ran the MR2, I knew he would toast me, but I wanted to play around. hehe||||reg||1
