Z||000035||DXR_WILL||10-02-1999||05:39 AM||||Performance wise and bang for your buck, is it worth it two rebuild the 2.8 V6 with Hi-Pro parts or swap engines with a Quad-4, 3800 Suprercharged, V8 etc.  I'm curious to hear what you all think, because my dad and I were debating the subject. ||||reg||1
Z||000036||batboy||10-02-1999||11:47 AM||||Obviously, an engine swap will give you more power in most cases, but the best bang for the buck would have to be rebuilding the 2.8. Even if you went hog wild on the V6 and stroked it, used forged pistons, headers, 70 shot NOS system, etc... it still will be cheaper than most of the engine swaps.||||reg||1
Z||000037||88 Fiero||10-02-1999||11:47 AM||||If you are planning to swap engines and willing to spend the bucks...swap it with a V8 and use either a TPI Corvette engine or the Northstar engine...Reason is I've given a ride in this cars and I'm tellin you it moves...specially the northstars..damn fast car..This guy that I know even uses the Cadillacs Tranny (it's a 4-Speed Auto) and the car really rocks!!!...plus it looks really good in the engine bay...||||reg||1
Z||000038||batboy||10-02-1999||12:21 PM||||There's no doubt that Northstar swap is a great way to go. The engine is compact, light weight, and with just basic mods, will produce over 300 HP. But, it is so expensive.<P>I heard a rumor that there is a Caddy V8 engine (not the Northstar) that will bolt up to the Fiero tranny. I can't remember the name of the car... Elantra or something like that. Is there a such a car or was being fed a bunch of bull?||||reg||1
Z||000039||terryk||10-02-1999||12:41 PM||||I vote for a 92-99 Buick 3800 SC running on the 92-95 ECM.  It fits the auto trans nicely. The manual trans is a bit tougher. <P>The Northstar is too expensive, the V8 swap is a kludge, the DOHC is too low on torque at the bottom for street racing. <P>IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000040||mwbackus||10-02-1999||01:36 PM||||The following information was obtained from Greg Duncan  <A HREF="mailto:gduncan@azstarnet.com">gduncan@azstarnet.com</A> <P>The `91-`93 Cadillac 4.9L V8! Aluminum block, cast iron heads, aluminum intake, port Fuel Injected and DOES NOT NEED AN ADAPTER PLATE! It bolts right up to all Fiero transaxles, but does require a custom flywheel and one chassis mod - a small portion of the right side hood hinge bracket must be removed. <BR>No hacking or chopping on the space frame or sub frame! When using an original Fiero transaxle, there is no relocating of the engine or trans needed, and no custom axles needed. And without A/C the engine weighs the same as a complete Fiero 2.8 V6 engine with A/C!!! <P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000041||batboy||10-02-1999||04:35 PM||||It must be the Cadillac Allante that I heard about. This almost sounds too good to be true, a 4.9L V8 that fits into the Fiero with only minor modification. I wonder what the HP and torque figures are on this engine. I bet it would be a very slick setup with an auto transmission. The down side is that these engines are probably ecpensive and hard to find.||||reg||1
Z||000042||mwbackus||10-02-1999||06:00 PM||||There are actually quite a few caddys that used this engine in that time frame.  I believe that the Seville even had one in 93.  My conclusions were that the flywheel would be a big drawback, unless one had the facilities to do this work, or could at least get it done for a decent price.  Performance parts for this motor may also be close to none existent, but I have no idea if any crossover parts would work, so I may be wrong there.  Greg seems like a pretty nice guy, and would be much more knowlegable on this swap than me since he has actually done one. Perhaps we could invite him to our forum to answer a few questions? :-)<P>Mike||||reg||1
